@startuml fixed-delta-commit-work
' ============================================================================
' The work of one trusted commit, as the fixed-delta scaling experiment of
' task 7929 (pgmcp experiment 446) measured it at commit 941ff07d: the four
' probed phases of the verify routine (ScalingChain::commit in
' rspace++/tests/persistence/trusted_scaling_properties.rs) and the five timed
' phases of the time child (run_timed in trusted_scaling_resource.rs). Each
' trial runs run_ledger_trial (rspace++/src/rspace/persistence/closure/
' ledger_trial.rs); the bind is bind_trusted_transaction in durable/admission.rs.
'   BLUE   #1565C0  sealing, staging, the radix edit and preparation
'   AMBER  #EF6C00  the ledger trials, which expand only the objects whose
'                   count crosses between 0 and 1
'   RED    #C62828  the measured cost that grows with the ledger (G4, H-O2)
'   GREEN  #2E7D32  the durable appends and the published successor
'   GREY   #607D8B  the release of shared persistent structures
'   TEAL   #00796B  what the probes read
'   PURPLE #6A1B9A  the negative controls, each where it acts
' ============================================================================
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.4
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #455A64
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #455A64
skinparam NoteBorderColor #455A64
skinparam PartitionBorderColor #455A64

title <b>The work of one trusted commit</b>\nthe objects, references and records that each step touches depend on the keys the commit changes,\nnot on the <latex>N</latex> keys or the <latex>H</latex> retained states; the bytes copied per touched ledger node do grow with the ledger

start
partition "seal" {
  :rewrite the search paths of the changed keys:
  loaded, edited and emitted nodes, exact path chunks;<<#BBDEFB>>
}
partition "bind" {
  :stage the sealed objects in memory
  over the core's pinned store;<<#BBDEFB>>
  repeat
    :next trial domain:
    current, retained, then metadata;<<#FFE0B2>>
    note right #FFF3E0
      <b>Objects each trial expands</b>
      current, adding <latex>T</latex> and removing <latex>B</latex>:
          <latex>X_{\mathrm{current}} = \mathrm{cl}(T) \mathbin{\triangle} \mathrm{cl}(B)</latex>
      retained, adding <latex>T</latex>: <latex>X_{\mathrm{retained}} = \mathrm{cl}(T) \setminus U</latex>
      metadata, adding <latex>a'</latex> and removing <latex>a</latex>:
          <latex>X_{\mathrm{metadata}} = (I' \mathbin{\triangle} I) \cup \{a, a'\}</latex>
      ----
      <latex>\mathrm{cl}(r)</latex>: the objects reachable from root <latex>r</latex>;
      <latex>U</latex>: the retained union; <latex>I, I'</latex>: the radix nodes
      before and after the edit; <latex>a, a'</latex>: the anchors.
      References: one per root event and one per
      out-reference of an expanded object.
    end note
    if (metadata domain?) then (yes)
      :V2 writer: path-local edit of the history radix
      at the predecessor's 48-byte key and a new
      136-byte anchor <latex>a'</latex>;<<#BBDEFB>>
      note left #F3E5F5
        <b>NC-V1</b>, the natural control, keeps the
        V1 writer: no radix edit and no metadata
        trial, but every commit rewrites the whole
        manifest, <latex>96 + 56n</latex> bytes for <latex>n</latex> entries.
      end note
    else (no)
    endif
    :authorize the root removals;<<#FFE0B2>>
    note left #F3E5F5
      <b>NC-SCAN</b> (revision r1) first loads, in the
      current trial, the object of every predecessor
      ledger entry: about <latex>|U|</latex> reads per commit, a
      CPU and read-byte cost that grows with <latex>N</latex>
      and <latex>H</latex>. The work counters do not see the
      reads, so G1 still passes.
    end note
    :clone the predecessor's ledger maps
    (a PathMap clone copies one root reference);<<#FFE0B2>>
    note right #F3E5F5
      <b>NC-CLONE</b> rebuilds the states map entry
      by entry: the allocations grow with the
      ledger, about <latex>|U|</latex> entries.
    end note
    :activation: add the roots and expand each object
    whose count crosses from 0 to 1;<<#FFE0B2>>
    :deactivation: remove the roots and expand each object
    whose count crosses from 1 to 0;<<#FFE0B2>>
    note right #F3E5F5
      <b>NC-ORDER</b> swaps the two phases: the current
      trial expands <latex>|\mathrm{cl}(B)| + |\mathrm{cl}(T)|</latex>, which
      exceeds <latex>|X_{\mathrm{current}}|</latex> by <latex>2\,|\mathrm{cl}(B) \cap \mathrm{cl}(T)|</latex>,
      so the verify routine's work check fails.
    end note
    :each state insertion copies the shared trie nodes
    on its 32-byte digest path (copy-on-write);<<#FFCDD2>>
    note left #FFEBEE
      <b>Measured: G4 fails on bytes (H-O2).</b>
      A level-1 node of a ledger with <latex>L</latex> entries holds about
      <latex>\min(L/256,\ 256)</latex> slots of 72 bytes, so a copied node
      costs up to about 18 KiB. The allocation-byte
      exponent is 0.413 on U and 0.319 on R; the
      allocation-count exponent stays within the bound
      (0.109 on U, 0.053 on R).
    end note
    :refuse at the first exceeded final inventory,
    else keep the successor ledger;<<#FFE0B2>>
  repeat while (another domain?) is (yes) not (no)
}
partition "prepare" {
  :classify the fresh objects against the committed index
  and frame the log-pack transaction;<<#BBDEFB>>
}
partition "publish" {
  :append the prepare header, the mutation, a record per
  fresh object and the commit record to the active pack,
  and one 72-byte index record per fresh object to the index;<<#C8E6C9>>
  :clone the committed index (a PathMap) and insert the new records,
  copy the retention manifest bytes (the 136-byte V2 anchor)
  and swap the catalog slot: no physical recovery,
  no cold admission and no index construction;<<#C8E6C9>>
}
partition "release (timed only)" {
  :drop the prepared and bound transactions
  and the predecessor core;<<#CFD8DC>>
}
stop

legend right
  <b>What the probes read</b>
  <back:#B2DFDB>verify mode</back>: around seal, bind, prepare and
  publish, the deltas of <i>/proc/self/io</i> and of the
  thread-local allocation counter, net of one I/O
  calibration per phase and one trace per commit
  <back:#B2DFDB>time mode</back>: six readings of the thread CPU,
  process CPU and wall clocks bound five phases;
  a process's statistic is the median, over its
  <latex>T</latex> timed commits, of their per-commit sum
endlegend
@enduml
